t*20Copilot*20Chat*20Files/infocom.pdf?web=3D1__;JSUl!!BhdT!hE_QRMqHbUObrfJ=
vnkR6vfiATP5UB4Q3maoh5shaQbkubeTruPluqIsaGNUheStVQAK58oHneQIpSSAUph6buQ$>

Case 3: Bayes-envelope exact form

The exact general object may not be a simple scalar [cid:image123.png@01DD1=
9E9.0FB00090] . It may be:
[cid:image124.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090]

This is the cleanest exact expression.

Then mutual information becomes the compressive summary or upper bound.

That gives you a hierarchy:
[cid:image125.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090]
[cid:image126.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090]
________________________________
6. Fifth upgrade: connect to Blackwell informativeness

This is where the paper becomes more control/information-theory mature.

Mutual information is not a complete ordering of information structures. Tw=
o observations can have the same mutual information but different usefulnes=
s for a particular decision problem. So the strongest formal framework is p=
robably:
[cid:image127.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090]

meaning [cid:image128.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090] is Blackwell more informative =
than [cid:image129.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090] . Then:
[cid:image130.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090]

for all utilities [cid:image131.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090] .

Then mutual information becomes a scalar relaxation:
[cid:image132.png@01DD19E9.0FB00090]

does not always imply Blackwell dominance, but it often gives useful bounds.

This would make your upgraded theory more defensible:

The exact control value is governed by the decision value of the delayed in=
formation structure; mutual information gives a universal scalar upper boun=
d and a closed-form equality in symmetric binary admission.


